os_linux_zero: don't redefine clang's __sync builtin

Zero supplies a ShouldNotCallThis() stub for __sync_val_compare_and_swap_8 on
32-bit targets, for platforms whose compiler emits a libcall for 64-bit atomics.
clang treats that name as one of its own builtins and refuses the definition:

  os_linux_zero.cpp:486:26: error: cannot redeclare builtin function
      '__sync_val_compare_and_swap_8'

Skip the stub under clang. It is not needed there: on the 32-bit ARM target
built here clang expands 64-bit compare-and-swap inline with LDREXD/STREXD
rather than calling out to a helper, and the stub only ever aborted anyway.

diff --git a/hotspot/src/os_cpu/linux_zero/vm/os_linux_zero.cpp b/hotspot/src/os_cpu/linux_zero/vm/os_linux_zero.cpp
index 84d29b2..4abf63e 100644
--- a/hotspot/src/os_cpu/linux_zero/vm/os_linux_zero.cpp
+++ b/hotspot/src/os_cpu/linux_zero/vm/os_linux_zero.cpp
@@ -481,7 +481,7 @@ extern "C" {
 // Implementations of atomic operations not supported by processors.
 //  -- http://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc-4.2.1/gcc/Atomic-Builtins.html
 
-#ifndef _LP64
+#if !defined(_LP64) && !defined(__clang__)
 extern "C" {
   long long unsigned int __sync_val_compare_and_swap_8(
     volatile void *ptr,
@@ -491,7 +491,7 @@ extern "C" {
     return 0; // silence compiler warnings
   }
 };
-#endif // !_LP64
+#endif // !_LP64 && !__clang__
 
 #ifndef PRODUCT
 void os::verify_stack_alignment() {
